  "parsed_answer": "The **CFTR gene** provides instructions for making the **cystic fibrosis transmembrane conductance regulator** protein, which is a channel that helps move **chloride ions** across cell membranes. This movement helps regulate the **balance of salt and water** on the surfaces of organs such as the lungs, pancreas, and intestines.\n\nIn **cystic fibrosis**, mutations in the CFTR gene cause the CFTR protein to work improperly or not at all. As a result:\n\n- **Less water** moves onto the cell surface\n- Mucus becomes **thick and sticky**\n- Airways and ducts can become **blocked**\n- This leads to **lung infections, breathing problems, digestive issues, and other complications**\n\nSo, the CFTR gene is central because its defective protein causes the abnormal mucus and organ problems characteristic of cystic fibrosis.",
